# EVERY EXPOSURE THAT HAD NOWHERE TO GO. One row per layer where every buffer is accounted for
# and none of them has room, beside the exposure its own two measurements imply.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE ARGUMENT IS THE WHOLE ROW AND NEVER ONE COLUMN. Read as "the capacity buffer is a
#    stated zero" this is one route closed rather than nowhere to go: the same layer could
#    still have drawn on stock or made the demand wait. Two rules rest on this
#    population, and from that premise both conclude that somebody went without, which does not
#    carry it. `S` is an L x 3 matrix and the three buffers are substitutes; the index belongs
#    in a column, which is where assets/ddl/schema.ddl has always kept it.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE ESCAPE CLAUSE THAT MAKES IT SOUND WAS ALREADY WRITTEN, FOR ONE BUFFER. "An absent
#    capacity slack licenses none of this, because nobody knows whether the supply could have
#    stretched." That is true of all three and was carried to none of the others.
#
# ✅⛔ AND IT RESTS ON `stated`, NOT ON `measured`. Once every zero carried a `boundOrigin` the
#    population could be read back, and none of the capacity zeros was measured: `contractual`,
#    a reserved block somebody negotiated, and `intrinsic`, a material limit. A shelf life
#    entails a zero, a negotiation fixes one, an observation records one, and all three are
#    somebody saying the buffer is empty and standing behind it.
#
# ⛔ TWO RULES READ THIS RELATION AND THEY ASK DIFFERENT QUESTIONS OF IT. One wants the layers
#   with no named holder; the other wants every one of them, against the shares that were
#   admitted. Inlined at both call sites, these joins are two copies of one population, which
#   is one edit away from two populations under one argument.
-- layers/exposure_scope.sqlc, restricted to the standing that licenses a conclusion.
SELECT s.*
FROM (
    :compose(layers/exposure_scope.sqlc)
) s
WHERE s.standing = 'every buffer sized and empty'
